## ABSTRACT

AI models have demonstrated the ability to analyze data, design experiments, and generate novel hypotheses, with the pace of progress convincing many researchers that AI could compete with science's greatest minds within decades. [Scientific American](https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/will-ai-ever-win-a-nobel-prize/) AlphaFold3 already predicts molecular interactions with improvements of at least 50% over prior methods, while GNoME has discovered 2.2 million new crystal structures, with 736 already physically synthesized by external researchers. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666389926000061) These are not incremental events. They mark the opening of a structural rupture between the speed of discovery and the capacity of legacy institutions to govern it.

The AI-Native Science Institution (AINS) is the response. It is a mathematically grounded, operationally specified, adversarially hardened framework for organizing scientific discovery, verification, attribution, risk governance, incentive design, and equity in a civilization where knowledge is produced jointly by humans and artificial agents.

Version 3.0 introduces: a discretized and computable objective function; a Cascade Integrity Layer for knowledge graph stability; a formal Adversarial Auditing Protocol that replaces passive human review; a Pre-Experimental Risk Screen that closes the dual-use loophole before dangerous experiments are run; a witness attestation layer that defeats provenance forgery; hardened DCU mechanics resistant to contribution farming and citation cartels; a dynamic and decaying equity multiplier; a Hybrid Consortia Deliberation Protocol with a formal decision lattice; an AI Alignment Safety Kernel; and an explicit roadmap section confronting institutional and geopolitical resistance.

What follows is not a vision. It is an engineering specification.

