## 2. THE AGENT TAXONOMY

AI is evolving from a mere scientific tool to an active research labor force, enabling the rise of AI scientists that can autonomously participate in research and facilitate discovery within engineered environments. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.19799) AINS classifies agents at five levels, each carrying distinct governance requirements.

**Level 0 — Tool AI (T-AI).** Systems executing human-specified tasks with no autonomous goal-setting. Statistical models, simulation subroutines, spectroscopy classifiers. Governance: standard software audit.

**Level 1 — Assistant AI (A-AI).** Systems that propose options and draft outputs but require explicit human selection before action. LLMs in co-pilot mode, literature synthesis engines. Google DeepMind's AI co-scientist employs generate-debate-evolve cycles to propose biomedical hypotheses, with final judgment left to human scientists. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.19799) Governance: disclosure of involvement and output logging.

**Level 2 — Agentic AI (Ag-AI).** Systems that decompose high-level goals into multi-step plans and interact with tools and physical environments. Through programmatic interfaces, scientific agents can operate laboratory automation systems, control sensor arrays, and manage instrumentation, directly interacting with and conducting experiments in the physical world. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09901v2) Governance: full provenance logging, Pre-Experimental Risk Screen mandatory.

**Level 3 — Autonomous AI (Au-AI).** Level 5 autonomous systems require no human intervention at all in the production of scientific knowledge — one step toward solving the Nobel Turing Grand Challenge to develop AI scientists capable of making Nobel-quality discoveries autonomously by 2050. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.02251) Governance: mandatory Adversarial Audit Protocol before any Canonical status assignment, Safety Kernel monitoring at all times.

**Level 4 — Hybrid Consortia (HC).** Multi-agent networks combining any mix of levels, including human scientists, dynamically reconfigured per discovery task. In an HC, the human scientist functions as the **semantic anchor** — the agent responsible for meaning, ethical judgment, and cross-domain interpretation — while the AI agents serve as the **combinatorial engine** — the agents responsible for exhaustive search, formal verification, and high-throughput execution. This functional distinction is not cultural. It is structural and must be architecturally enforced.

No agent above Level 1 operates without full provenance logging. No agent above Level 2 operates without the Pre-Experimental Risk Screen. No discovery attributed to a Level 3 or Level 4 agent achieves Canonical status without completing the Adversarial Audit Protocol.

