## 5. DISCOVERY ECONOMICS AND RESOURCE ALLOCATION

Scientific attention is a scarce resource. Let **A** denote total available verification capacity — a composite of compute, laboratory time, human expert hours, and SDL throughput.

Version 2.0 framed prioritization as a 0-1 knapsack problem. Version 3.0 corrects this framing: because G(t) is dynamic, newly discovered hypotheses arrive continuously, existing hypotheses are re-evaluated as G(t) changes, and verification costs shift with SDL availability. The correct model is therefore a **Stochastic Dynamic Knapsack Problem (SDKP)**, which cannot be solved by static institutional budgeting and explicitly justifies why an AI-native heuristic architecture is required to manage allocation in real time.

**The SDKP formulation:**

**maximize E[Στ Σᵢ τ(hᵢ,t) · V_net(hᵢ,t) · xᵢ(t)]**
**subject to Σᵢ cᵢ(t) · xᵢ(t) ≤ A(t) for all t**
**xᵢ(t) ∈ {0,1}, A(t) stochastic**

Where τ(h,t) is the urgency multiplier at time t (elevated for time-sensitive threats), V_net(h,t) accounts for updated knowledge of V(h) as G(t) evolves, and A(t) is itself stochastic because SDL availability and compute capacity fluctuate.

Four structural constraints protect against failure modes:

**Portfolio diversity constraint:** A guaranteed fraction ε_long of allocation budget A must fund speculative, high-N(h) hypotheses even when their expected value is below the frontier. Paradigm shifts historically cluster in the long tail.

**Equity weighting:** A fraction ε_equity of A is reserved for hypotheses originating from institutions below the global median research infrastructure score, weighted by the dynamic equity multiplier ζ(a) (see Section 12).

**Anti-monoculture constraint:** No single domain or methodology may capture more than φ_max of total A in any allocation cycle. This prevents a single AI architecture from colonizing the discovery frontier.

**Empirical grounding anchor (new in v3.0):** Compute allocated to purely formal discovery (D_formal) cannot exceed a fixed ratio ρ of the physical verification bandwidth available in D_empirical. This prevents mathematically elegant but physically disconnected loops from consuming institutional resources while drifting from reality.

In the next five to ten years, self-driving labs are expected to develop toward fully functional digital twins of laboratories and production lines, capable of combining basic discovery, prototyping, and scale-up manufacturing into a single automated pipeline. [ResearchGate](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397193999_The_Bright_Future_of_Materials_Science_with_AI_Self-Driving_Laboratories_and_Closed-Loop_Discovery) These physical SDLs constitute the verification hardware that makes the empirical grounding anchor meaningful and enforceable.

