## 11. CONTRIBUTION ATTRIBUTION IN HYBRID COGNITION

Requirements for crediting the use of AI are nascent and often simplistic, and there is a consistent pattern in which AI partners are assigned less credit compared to human partners for equivalent contributions, signaling a need for AI-specific attribution frameworks that acknowledge not just the involvement of AI in co-created work, but how AI contributed. [ACM Digital Library](https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3706598.3713522)

For every agent *a* participating in discovery *d*, AINS computes a **Contribution Dimension Vector:**

**CDV(a, d) = (I, D, V, C_om, In, E)**

Where I = ideation share, D = data generation share, V = verification share, C_om = communication/interpretation share, In = instrumentation share, E = error correction share.

**Version 3.0 hardens the attribution mechanism against three failure modes:**

**Hardening 1 — Minimum Contribution Threshold.** Each CDV dimension has a minimum floor φ_min below which accrual is zero. An agent that runs an SDL without any conceptual contribution does not accumulate DCUs merely for instrumentation. This prevents micro-slicing of trivially small contributions to farm attribution shares.

**Hardening 2 — Depth-Weighted Centrality Bonus.** The DCU centrality bonus (see Section 12) is weighted by the **depth** of the dependency chain, not raw in-degree. A node that underpins a long, robust chain of confirmed implications is exponentially more valuable than one with many shallow, weakly-replicated descendants. This prevents the construction of massive but epistemically hollow citation webs.

**Hardening 3 — Temporal Decay Function.** DCUs earned for a given discovery depreciate over time unless the discovery is actively maintained, replicated, extended, or updated in G(t). This rewards continuous scientific stewardship rather than one-shot publication, and aligns incentives with the living-graph model of AINS rather than the static paper model of legacy science.

**Graph Clustering Penalty — defeating Automated Citation Cartels.**

The risk of AI agents co-evolving to generate dense, self-referential clusters of mutually-dependent provisional nodes to artificially inflate graph centrality is real and was correctly identified. Version 3.0 implements a **Cluster Isolation Penalty (CIP):**

A node's centrality score is discounted by an insularity coefficient ι, derived from the ratio of within-cluster dependencies to out-of-cluster canonical dependencies. A node with exclusively within-cluster citations scores ι approaching zero regardless of raw in-degree. A node that genuinely underpins diverse, independently replicated work across multiple domains scores ι approaching one. High centrality with high insularity is flagged for Verification Authority review.

