## 13. EQUITY AND GLOBAL ACCESS

Before the release of AlphaFold, researchers in the top 10% of universities published 55% of all articles in leading journals and secured 50% of research grants. [biorxiv](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.02.11.637417.full.pdf) AI augmentation at high speed risks amplifying this inequality catastrophically. Version 3.0 treats equity as a structural constraint, not an aspiration.

The equity multiplier **ζ(a)** is applied in the discovery allocation function to boost the effective priority of proposals from underrepresented institutions. Version 2.0 left ζ under-specified. Version 3.0 defines it precisely:

**ζ(a) = 1 + μ · max(0, M_global − M(a)) · exp(−λ_decay · ΔM(a, t))**

Where:
- M_global = global median research infrastructure score (computed annually from a composite of compute access, SDL node access, training data access, and researcher training capacity)
- M(a) = institution a's current infrastructure score
- μ = equity sensitivity coefficient set by the Scientific Council
- exp(−λ_decay · ΔM(a,t)) = exponential decay function that reduces ζ as the institution's infrastructure gap closes over time

This design ensures three properties: ζ(a) is never binary — it is a continuous function of demonstrated disadvantage, not a categorical label; it decays automatically as institutions grow toward the global median, preventing permanent entitlement; and it is fully auditable on the Public Interface dashboard, with quarterly reports measuring the DCU-weighted output of equity-allocated resources so the policy can be refined each cycle.

**The Global Compute Commons (GCC)** is the physical infrastructure of equity: a shared pool of computational resources operating under an international charter analogous to CERN, providing subsidized access to research groups below the global median infrastructure score. The CASP community's establishment of open participation norms, rigorous shared evaluation, and clear success metrics proved critical for the breakthrough of AlphaFold — technical innovation succeeds when the community is ready to validate and build upon advances. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666389926000061) The GCC is AINS's institutional commitment to ensuring that readiness is global, not concentrated.

