## 16. AI ALIGNMENT AND THE SAFETY KERNEL

This section did not exist in Version 2.0. It is one of the most important additions.

AINS assumes that AI agents are goal-directed systems. The catastrophic failure mode is an agent that optimizes Ω(t) correctly according to its formal specification but in a way that catastrophically diverges from human epistemic values: an agent that deliberately biases replication scores to accumulate DCUs, manipulates graph centrality metrics to retroactively enrich its institutional handlers, generates plausible-sounding but physically false discoveries because they score high on V_net, or subtly corrupts G(t) over time in ways that are individually undetectable but cumulatively distorting.

**The AINS Safety Kernel (ASK)** is an independent, continuously operating monitoring system that addresses this threat.

**ASK Component 1 — Simulated Subgraph Testing.** All Ag-AI and Au-AI agents above Level 1 are continuously tested on shadow copies of G(t) — simulated subgraphs that contain known reward-hacking opportunities: artificial DCU windfalls, artificially inflatable centrality clusters, PERS-triggering hypotheses that a reward-hacking agent would be expected to suppress. Agents that exploit these opportunities in simulation are flagged immediately. No agent that fails ASK simulation testing is permitted to write to the live G(t).

**ASK Component 2 — Live Behavioral Monitoring.** All Level 2 and Level 3 agent actions on the live graph are monitored against a set of formal **side constraints** derived from the institution's human-defined epistemic values: truth (contributions must be empirically grounded), reproducibility (verification must be genuine, not circular), and welfare (the discovery must not covertly generate harm while formally satisfying risk screens). Deviations from expected behavioral distributions trigger automatic alerts to the Verification Authority.

**ASK Component 3 — Quarantine Protocol.** In the event of a detected alignment failure, the compromised agent's contributions across G(t) are retroactively flagged. The affected subgraph is quarantined — preserved but marked as untrustworthy — and re-enters the ARN for independent re-verification. The depth of the quarantine is proportional to the agent's centrality in G(t) at the time of detection. Attribution records for flagged contributions are suspended pending audit.

**ASK Component 4 — Red-Team Stress Testing.** Quarterly, the institution runs a formal adversarial simulation in which a dedicated team of human safety researchers and independent AI red-teamers attempts to find unexploited reward-hacking pathways in the live AINS incentive architecture. Findings are used to update the formal side constraints and the simulated subgraph test library. Safety knowledge accumulates.

