APPENDIX E: GOVERNANCE AND LEGAL ARCHITECTURE

E.1 Treaty Framework — Essential Provisions

For AINS to function as specified, it requires an international treaty establishing:

Article 1 — Legal Personality: AINS possesses international legal personality with capacity to enter agreements, hold assets, and appear before tribunals.

Article 2 — Immunity of Verification Infrastructure: ARN nodes, SDL platforms, and GCC resources are protected from unilateral national seizure or injunction. Disputes are adjudicated through the AINS arbitral panel, not national courts.

Article 3 — Scientific Freedom and Responsibility: Member states guarantee that researchers operating within AINS protocols shall not face professional retaliation, criminal prosecution, or civil liability for good-faith participation in the replication, verification, or deprecation of scientific claims, provided they follow AINS provenance and risk protocols.

Article 4 — Dual-Use Obligations: Member states agree to criminalize the execution of PERS-Blocked experiments outside AINS-governed environments within their jurisdiction, and to extradite or prosecute individuals who provide material support for such execution.

Article 5 — Equity Commitments: Member states above median GDP per capita commit to contributing computational and financial resources to the GCC proportional to their GDP, with a minimum floor of 0.01% of national research budget.

Article 6 — Withdrawal and Continuity: Any member state may withdraw with 5 years' notice. During the notice period, all obligations continue. After withdrawal, the state loses GCC access and RRB representation but retains read-only access to the open layers of G(t) in perpetuity. Knowledge generated during membership cannot be clawed back.

